  "text": "The gathering began after the last boat returned and Mara had helped its passengers ashore. She crossed to the garden with her work finished. This time, she could read The Torn Wing there herself.\n\nPip crossed the river with a way back behind him. In the garden, he and Grandma planted the seed she had given him. He had kept his promise. A lantern-flower grew where they had worked together.\n\nMara was still working at the dock when the gathering began. With her permission, Pip read The Torn Wing from her page. Her story reached the garden while she stayed with the passengers.\n\n“Are you waiting for someone?” I asked. He shook his head. One wing of the bird was torn. Its tip hung lower than the other, and his thumb rested just below the tear.\n\nWe’ve postponed the gathering until the last boat returns. Grandma has agreed, so Mara can finish helping the passengers before she joins us. Please bring your story.\n\nI reached toward the bird. He pulled it closer. My hand stopped between us. For a moment, neither of us said anything. Water tapped against the side of the boat.\n\nWhen he stepped onto the dock, he carried the bird carefully in both hands. At the last step he paused, then found a clear place for his foot.\n\nSol brought his unfinished page. At the gathering, Pip helped him add an ending about what followed the repair, and Sol shared that part too.\n\nSol read A Small Repair and the ending he and Pip had prepared. The others listened to an account he had once thought too ordinary to bring.\n\n“Would you like some help?” I asked. “We could look at it together.” He hesitated. Then he held out the bird, with both hands underneath it.\n\n“Now I can tell my grandmother how we fixed it,” he said. He tilted the bird once, very slightly, and the mended wing caught the light.",
